PART 2. ABUSES FOUND AND HOW THEY WERE FIXED
======================================================================

#1  CRITICAL: anyone could read the admin pages (customer names, orders, sales)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Where:   src/app/admin/layout.tsx, all 11 pages under src/app/admin/
Problem: Only the admin LAYOUT checked the ADMIN role. When the browser navigates inside
         the app, Next.js only renders the page, not the layouts the browser says it
         already has. A hand-made request that claims "I already have the admin layout"
         (headers `RSC: 1` + `Next-Router-State-Tree`) skips the check completely.
How it was proven (live, production build, anonymous visitor, no login):
  Before the fix:
    GET /admin/orders (normal)                 -> 307 redirect to /login  (looks safe)
    GET /admin/orders (crafted RSC request)    -> 200, 9257 bytes, containing:
        the total sales figure ("140,000 DT"), the order status tabs,
        real customer names (redacted here) and links to 7 real orders
    /admin/products, /admin/reviews, /admin/shipments -> 200 with the admin page as well
  After the fix (same crafted requests, new build):
    /admin/orders       -> redirect to /login, no data
    /admin/products     -> redirect to /login, no data
    /admin/reviews      -> redirect to /login, no data
    /admin/shipments    -> redirect to /login, no data
    /admin/categories   -> redirect to /login, no data
    /admin/shipments/new, /admin/products/new -> redirect to /login, no data
    normal GET /admin/orders -> 307 /login; home page -> 200 (nothing else broken)
Fix:     every admin page now calls `await requirePageRole("ADMIN")` itself, as its first
         line (the layout keeps its check too). Server actions were already safe: each
         one calls requireRole itself (proven by src/actions/access.test.ts).
Guard:   src/test/access-rules.test.ts fails if a new admin page forgets the check.

#2  MEDIUM: the per-product quantity limit (20) could be bypassed
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Where:   src/services/orders.ts, mergeItems()
Problem: The limit was checked on each cart line BEFORE duplicate lines were merged. A
         hand-made checkout request with the same product 10 times x 20 ordered 200 units
         in one order (for example to lock all the stock of an IEM with one guest order).
Fix:     the limit is checked on the merged quantity. A cart may have at most 50 lines
         (same as the cart page), so a request with thousands of lines is refused
         before any database query.
Tests:   "[fixed] does not let the same product be split into many lines..."
         "[fixed] refuses a cart with an absurd number of lines"

#3  MEDIUM: an order could be cancelled after the admin confirmed its payment
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Where:   src/services/orders.ts, setStatus()
Problem: setStatus read the order, then updated it by id alone. If the customer pressed
         "Cancel" while the admin was marking the order Paid, both succeeded: the paid
         order ended up CANCELLED and its stock was put back. The fake-database test
         showed the customer's cancel overwrote PAID.
Fix:     compare-and-swap on the status (same pattern as the stock code): the update only
         matches if the status is still the one that was read, otherwise the customer
         gets "This order just changed, please reload the page" (new text in English and
         French: errors.orderChanged). The stock is put back only after the status change
         succeeded, inside the same transaction.
Tests:   "[fixed] a customer's cancel can't undo a payment the admin just confirmed"
Note:    a double-clicked cancel was NOT a bug: part 3 shows its test already passed on
         the original code, because the stock compare-and-swap made the second cancel
         fail. Its test is kept (without the [fixed] tag) so it stays that way.

#4  LOW: no length limit on the checkout fields
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Where:   src/services/orders.ts (validateContact), src/components/cart/CheckoutFields.tsx
Problem: name, email, address, city, postal code and notes had no server-side limit (the
         form limited notes only in the browser). A script could store pages of text in
         an order. The name is repeated in the emails the shop sends ("Hi <name>") and in
         the admin's new-order email, so the shop's own mail server could be used to send
         long spam text to any address typed as the customer email.
Fix:     server limits: name 80, email 254, address 160, city 60, postal code 12, notes 500
         characters, with a translated message naming the field (errors.detailTooLong).
         The same limits are set as maxLength on the checkout inputs (no visual change).
Tests:   "[fixed] refuses pages of text in the checkout fields..."
         "names the too-long field in the customer's language"

#5  LOW: very long account names shown on public reviews
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Where:   src/services/reviews.ts, submit()
Problem: the review author name is the account name, which has no length limit at sign-up
         (better-auth). It was copied onto the public product page as-is.
Fix:     the name is cut to 60 characters before saving (and before masking).
Test:    "[fixed] cuts a very long account name before showing it publicly"

#6  LOW (hardening): the after-login redirect accepted "/\evil.tn"
----------------------------------------------------------------------
Where:   src/lib/after-login.ts, readAfterLogin()
Problem: the check refused "//evil.tn" but accepted "/\evil.tn", which browsers turn into
         https://evil.tn/. Not exploitable today: the only value ever stored is "/cart".
Fix:     backslash refused as well.
Test:    "never sends people to another site"

#7  (found while testing, not a code bug) my own first test run was wrong
----------------------------------------------------------------------
While re-running the live attack after the fix, the new server failed to start because
the OLD server (pre-fix build) was still running on port 3100: my stop command had not
matched its process name. The first "after" results were therefore from the old build and
were thrown away; the server was stopped by its PID and the attack repeated on the new
build (results above). Noted here so the log is complete.

Checked and found SAFE (no change needed):
  - Nobody can make themselves ADMIN: `role` is `input: false` in the auth config.
  - All 24 admin server actions refuse guests, customers and STAFF (tested one by one).
  - Prices are always recomputed on the server; a price sent by the browser is ignored.
  - The browser can't choose whose order it is: the user id comes from the session.
  - Stock can't go below zero with two buyers at once (compare-and-swap, tested).
  - Guest order links use a 128-bit token compared in constant time.
  - Chat: only the order's owner and admins can read, write or load photos; photos are
    checked by their bytes (no SVG); text and photos are encrypted at rest.
  - Public tracking page: name, phone and address are masked on the server; internal
    notes, AliExpress link and contact channel are never sent.
  - Emails: everything a customer types is HTML-escaped; an email with a line break
    (header injection) is refused.
  - Reviews: only customers with a paid order can review; one review per product.
  - Product and shipment forms only accept http(s) links (no javascript: or data: links).

